-- Q1. What is a trigger in PL/SQL?
-- Ans: Trigger ek stored PL/SQL block hai jo
-- specified database event par automatically execute hota hai.

-- Q2. What is the purpose of this trigger?
-- Ans: Ye Department 10 ke employees ko delete hone se rokta hai.

-- Q3. What does BEFORE DELETE mean?
-- Ans: Employee delete hone se pehle trigger execute hota hai.

-- Q4. What is :OLD.DEPTNO?
-- Ans: Ye delete hone wale employee ka existing department number batata hai.

-- Q5. What is RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR?
-- Ans: Iska use custom Oracle error number aur message
-- generate karne ke liye hota hai.

-- Q6. Why is -20001 used?
-- Ans: Ye Oracle ke user-defined application error
-- range -20000 se -20999 ke andar aata hai.

-- Q7. What happens when we delete an employee from Department 10?
-- Ans: Trigger error raise karta hai aur employee delete nahi hota.

-- Q8. Can employees from other departments be deleted?
-- Ans: Haan, ye trigger sirf Department 10 ki deletion rokta hai.

-- Q9. What is :OLD?
-- Ans: :OLD DELETE ya UPDATE se pehle ki row values access karne deta hai.

-- Q10. What is FOR EACH ROW?
-- Ans: Isse trigger har affected employee row ke liye execute hota hai.

-- Q11. Does a trigger need to be called manually?
-- Ans: Nahi, DELETE statement par trigger automatically fire hota hai.

-- Q12. What is the purpose of SHOW ERRORS?
-- Ans: Isse trigger compile karte waqt aayi errors check kar sakte hain.

-- Q13. What is the difference between BEFORE and AFTER triggers?
-- Ans: BEFORE trigger operation se pehle aur AFTER trigger operation ke baad execute hota hai.

-- Q14. Can the protected department be changed?
-- Ans: Haan, condition mein 10 ki jagah required department number likh sakte hain.

-- Q15. What happens if the DELETE statement affects no rows?
-- Ans: Koi row delete nahi hoti, isliye row-level trigger execute nahi hota.
